> Included several suspcious items including:

I'm only commenting on a few arguable ones - such features _could_
show up in a legitimate game. What makes them suspicious is the
combination.

> 1) +8 bullwhip... and a +0 crysknife and +1 Mojo (why enchant the
> bullwhip?)

This could simply be due to playing a deliberately-restricted game. A
+8 bullwhip is about as good as grand mastery in martial arts - unless
you're facing something thick-skinned. As only really enchanted weapon
in a game with -2 AC on the Astral Plane it's not very plausible,
granted.

Mojo was +2. More interestingly, Mojo wasn't rustproof, which suggests
it was wished-for or found; no other "giftable" artefacts are to be
seen. No weapon is listed as being wielded, and the Mjöllnir is also
not mentioned among the possessions in the final "You went to your
reward with ..." list.

> 2) wore a helm of opposite alignment, but had the whole plane mapped out

Could still be rationalised away by assuming the player _wanted_ to
ascend with a different alignment.

> 3) Horrible AC (-2) wouldn't be survivable on Astral, even if you geno'd
> almost *everything* ;)

I haven't tried myself (worst AC i've had on Astral was -5, i think),
but i wouldn't consider beating the Astral Plane at -2 AC impossible.
It'd take a lot of skill and a better weapon than a +2 Mjöllnir or +8
bullwhip, though. 

> 4) Hardly any turns -- under 9000 -- but:
> a) was XL 29 with ~600 hp, 1200 pw
> b) killed about 3000 monsters

Yep. That was the dead giveaway for me. It just doesn't compute in a
normal (or even explore-mode) game.

> 9) wearing +5 GoD, had 15 dex.  As a ranger.

Well, yes. Probably kept losing Dx due to going Satiated for excessive
lengths of time. Nothing unusual for an inexperienced player, i should
think. Doesn't make the ascension any more plausible, of course, but
nothing suspicious, either.

> 13) genocided centaurs, water mocccasins, and *soldiers* (they're @
> right?)

Doesn't prove a thing. Soldiers can be genocided with an uncursed
genocide scroll. Centaurs are a weak but reasonable genocide choice
for a ranger - their quest is brimming with them. The genocides are a
bit strange (look typical for a newbie having trouble in the earlier
stages of the game) but not at all suspicious.

14) the intrinsics list doesn't look like it's been tampered with, and
even though it mentions magic-protection in spite of no MR-granting
item showing up in the equipment list, _there's not a single
resistance to be seen there_. No reflection, either.

